## Named Templates: The DRY Principle in Helm

Every Kubernetes resource in your chart needs the same set of labels: the app name, the chart version, the release name, the managed-by indicator. Without named templates, you copy-paste this block into every template file. When label requirements change — adding a cost-center label, renaming a field — you update every file and risk missing one.

Named templates solve this with the same principle as functions in any programming language: define once, call everywhere. A named template is defined with `{{- define "name" -}}` in `_helpers.tpl` and invoked with `{{- include "name" . }}` in any other template file.

**Why `_helpers.tpl` starts with an underscore:** Helm renders every `.yaml` and `.tpl` file in `templates/` as a Kubernetes manifest, then attempts to apply it. A file that contains only Go `define` blocks with no surrounding YAML would produce an empty document — which Kubernetes rejects. The underscore prefix is Helm's signal to skip rendering this file. It is loaded for its `define` blocks but never emitted as output.

**The `fullname` helper** demonstrates a common pattern: the release name and chart name are combined to produce the resource name, but capped at 63 characters (Kubernetes label and name length limit) and trimmed of trailing dashes. This logic would be tedious to repeat in every resource. Defining it once in `_helpers.tpl` ensures consistency and makes it easy to add `fullnameOverride` support for users who need a specific name.

**Selector labels vs standard labels — an important distinction:** Selector labels (used in `matchLabels` and service selectors) are **immutable after first install**. Kubernetes refuses to update a Deployment's `spec.selector` field. If you add a new label to your selector labels helper, every existing Deployment in every cluster using this chart will fail to upgrade — you must delete and recreate it. This is why selector labels are a stable, minimal subset (typically just `app.kubernetes.io/name` and `app.kubernetes.io/instance`), while the full labels block can include chart version, managed-by, and any other metadata that can safely change.

**The `trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-"` pattern** appears repeatedly in `_helpers.tpl` because Kubernetes imposes a 63-character limit on label values and names, and a truncated name should not end with a dash (which would be invalid).

Named templates are reusable snippets — define once, use everywhere. This is how you avoid copy-pasting the same labels into every resource.

```yaml
# templates/_helpers.tpl

{{/*
Expand the name of the chart.
*/}}
{{- define "my-app.name" -}}
{{- default .Chart.Name .Values.nameOverride | trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-" }}
{{- end }}

{{/*
Create a default fully qualified app name.
*/}}
{{- define "my-app.fullname" -}}
{{- if .Values.fullnameOverride }}
{{- .Values.fullnameOverride | trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-" }}
{{- else }}
{{- $name := default .Chart.Name .Values.nameOverride }}
{{- printf "%s-%s" .Release.Name $name | trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-" }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}

{{/*
Common labels — applied to all resources for consistency
*/}}
{{- define "my-app.labels" -}}
helm.sh/chart: {{ include "my-app.chart" . }}
app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ include "my-app.name" . }}
app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}
app.kubernetes.io/version: {{ .Chart.AppVersion | quote }}
app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: {{ .Release.Service }}
{{- if .Values.extraLabels }}
{{ toYaml .Values.extraLabels }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}

{{/*
Selector labels — stable subset, don't change after first install
*/}}
{{- define "my-app.selectorLabels" -}}
app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ include "my-app.name" . }}
app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}
{{- end }}

{{/*
Create chart name+version as used by the chart label.
*/}}
{{- define "my-app.chart" -}}
{{- printf "%s-%s" .Chart.Name .Chart.Version | replace "+" "_" | trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-" }}
{{- end }}
```

Using named templates:
```yaml
# In any template file
labels:
  {{- include "my-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
```
